{"id": "6588", "revid": "36420", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=6588", "title": "Central Tanzania", "text": "Central Tanzania is the country's plateau region, with grasslands and national parks in its south and arable land in the north.\nUnderstand.\nCentral Tanzania is characterised by broad plateaus, dry savanna, semi-arid landscapes and important administrative centres. The area includes the national capital, Dodoma, as well as towns such as Singida and Tabora, and serves as a link between Tanzania's coastal regions and the western and northern parts of the country.\nCentral Tanzania is largely situated on the East African Plateau. Much of the region has a drier climate than the humid coastal areas, and the landscape is dominated by grassland, savanna, woodland and cultivated land.\nAgriculture and livestock keeping are important economic activities, while administrative services, trade and transportation are concentrated in the larger towns. The region is also culturally diverse, with several ethnic communities maintaining distinctive languages and traditions.\nDodoma is the political centre of Tanzania and has grown significantly as government institutions have been transferred from Dar es Salaam.\nClimate.\nCentral Tanzania generally has a tropical savanna to semi-arid climate, depending on location and elevation. Rainfall is seasonal and is usually concentrated in a single rainy season, followed by a long dry period.\nDays can be hot, particularly during the dry season, while nights may be considerably cooler because of the region's elevation and distance from the coast.\nGet around.\nBuses and shared minibuses are widely used for travel between towns and villages. Taxis and private vehicles are useful within larger urban centres."}
{"id": "6589", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=6589", "title": "Central Tennessee", "text": "Central Tennessee, more often called Middle Tennessee, is a region of Tennessee. Besides the obvious destinations like Nashville, there are some lesser known ones, like Carthage, for instance, the hometown of former Vice-President Al Gore.\nDrink.\nVisit the Microbrewery at Yazoo in Nashville. Take the tour get a growler"}
{"id": "6591", "revid": "2240936", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=6591", "title": "Central Thailand", "text": "Central Thailand, also known as the Central Plains, is the low-lying central region of Thailand. It is centered around the giant urban agglomeration of Bangkok.\nRegions.\nCentral Thailand can be divided into three regions:\nUnderstand.\nThis region has long been the Thai heartland. Its geography is near-perfect: a fertile, flat basin ideal for growing prodigious amounts of rice, with rivers for irrigation and transport. The abundance of food and the dense population resulting from this created a tax base which sustained the development of the Thai state from the 13th century Sukhothai Kingdom to contemporary Bangkok.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nMost visitors to Thailand will arrive at Suvarnabhumi Airport, Thailand's largest airport 15 km (19 miles) east of Bangkok. It is roughly a 45 minute ride from the centre of Bangkok, but heavy congestion could make that trip into 1\u00bd hours or more. More information about the airport can be found in the Bangkok article.\nThe Airport Rail Link connects Suvarnabhumi Airport with stations in the city, including Makkasan and Phaya Thai where you can interchange with city rail lines.\nYou can also wait in line at the queue for a metered taxi at the first floor; a trip to the city costs about 250-450 baht. Always use the official taxi stand, though, and make sure the meter is turned on; otherwise, you might get in trouble later on.\nBy car.\nThe Central Plains has good road connections with the east, south, north and northeast of the country.\nTwo main roads connect the Central Plains with Eastern Thailand. The main road is Sukhumvit Road, also known as Route 3, which starts in Bangkok and via Pattaya reaches all the way to Trat and the Cambodian border. Another route goes east to Aranyaprathet from which the border with Cambodia can be crossed to Siem Reap and Angkor Archaeological Park (but don't even think about driving on the Cambodian part).\nFrom Phuket, Krabi Province and other destinations in Southern Thailand, take Route 4 that enters the Central Plains right after Chumphon. Passing Hua Hin, Cha-am and Samut Songkhram, the long ride reaches Bangkok.\nGet around.\nBecause the Thai rail network is centred on Bangkok, this is the best region of Thailand to explore via train. Railways extend west to Kanchanaburi, south to Hua Hin and beyond, north to Ayutthaya and points north and east, as well as destinations surrounding Bangkok like the Mae Klong Railway Market in Samut Songkram.\nSee.\nThere is a lot to see in the central region. Most visitors arrive in Bangkok, a true treasure house that literally has hundreds of sights. Prime sights are the Grand Palace, Wat Pho, Wat Arun and Jim Thompson's House, but it is just a small fragment of its historical and cultural heritage. The Bangkok Metropolitan Area is less inviting for foreigners, but there are some sights you can see. Samut Prakan has some interesting museums, or you could take a boat ride north to Nonthaburi, or even better, Ko Kret.\nThere are many day-trips you can undertake from Bangkok. The Summer Palace at Bang Pa-In makes for a nice stop on the way to Ayutthaya. Ayutthaya was the capital of Siam for 400 years and its historical remains should not be missed. There are literally hundreds of structures spread over and around the island, so renting a bicycle or moped is recommended. A short train ride north brings you to Lop Buri, a smaller historical town known for its macaques. You can take the sleeper train from here to Northern Thailand.\nThere are endless fields of shrimp farms and rice paddies West of Bangkok, and of course the floating markets. The most popular among foreign visitors is Damnoen Saduak, but the locals prefer to visit Amphawa or Tha Kha. Samut Songkhram makes a good base for getting to the floating markets. A scenic way to get here is with the Mae Khlong Railway, a slow and rustic train that requires a boat transfer at the fishing village of Samut Sakhon. Nakhon Pathom makes a nice half-day visit for the Phra Pathom Chedi, the world's tallest stupa.\nGoing west, the terrain becomes more hilly as you get into the River Kwai Valleys. Kanchanaburi is another popular stop on the tourist trail for the Bridge over the River Kwai and its World War II Museums. If you have a few days to spend in this area, some of the interesting attractions include the Erawan Falls and Hellfire Pass. A popular activity is to take a train ride over the bridge, just for the heck of it. You could even go all the way to Sangkhlaburi and Three Pagodas Pass, a bus ride of four hours to the border with Myanmar.\nIf you're heading for the south, the Northern Gulf Coast has a lot to offer visitors. There are the beach resorts of Hua Hin, Cha-am and Ban Krut. Phetchaburi has a charming old quarter, and is often visited as a day-trip from Bangkok, Hua Hin or Cha-am. Hua Hin is also home to the waterfalls of the Kaeng Krachan National Park and the natural scenery of the Khao Sam Roi Yot National Park. Finally, you could continue the way to the beaches and islands of Southern Thailand.\nEat.\nThai food (street food)"}
